{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the ruler of the County of Tripoli from 1149 until his death in 1152. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and the brother of Baldwin III Count of Tripoli. Raymond was born around 1115 and married Sibylla of Jerusalem in 1142.\n\n## **Early Life and Reign**\n\nRaymond III was the eldest son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Sancha of Alpilles. He was educated by the Normans in the court of King Stephen of Blois and also by the French knights in the court of King Louis VII of France. Raymond became the Count of Tripoli in 1149 after the death of his brother Baldwin III. He was a skilled military commander and led the County of Tripoli to several victories against the Fatimid Caliphate.\n\n## **Conflict with the Kingdom of Jerusalem**\n\nRaymond III was at odds with King Baldwin III of Jerusalem over the governance of the County of Tripoli. The two brothers disagreed on the extent of the County's autonomy and the role of the Kingdom of Jerusalem in its governance. The conflict between the two brothers led to a civil war in 1149 which ended with the Treaty of Damascus. The treaty established a seven-year truce between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli with Raymond III as the ruler of the County.\n\n## **Death and Legacy**\n\nRaymond III died in 1152 without an heir. He was succeeded by his cousin Emico of Hauteville who was a member of the Hauteville family a powerful Norman family. Raymond's death led to a period of instability in the County of Tripoli which was eventually resolved with the accession of Raymond's cousin Amalric I who went on to unite the County of Tripoli with the Kingdom of Jerusalem.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0016098171472549438}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001308832288486883}, {"token": " Count",